# HFGS-fPINN: Fourier-induced physics-informed neural networks with hard constraints and gated singular features for spatiotemporal fractional nonlocal heat diffusion equations

## Abstract
Existing fractional physics-informed neural networks (fPINNs) have made progress in approximating nonlocal operators through numerical discretization. However, their application to complex problems remains largely unexplored, such as the spatiotemporal fractional nonlocal heat diffusion equations with initial weak singularities, boundary low-regularity structures and complex internal variations. Moreover, despite the growing body of fPINNs literature, rigorous fixed-grid error analysis of fully discrete schemes remains scarce. In this paper, we propose a hard-constrained Fourier-induced gated singular-feature fPINN (HFGS-fPINN) for solving the spatiotemporal fractional nonlocal heat diffusion equations. The proposed method integrates a Fourier feature mapping branch with a gated singular-feature embedding to enhance the representation of multiscale frequency components and to improve the joint approximation of boundary low-regularity structures, initial weak singularities, and complex internal variations. A spatiotemporal output with hard constraints is further constructed to suppress the propagation and accumulation of initial and boundary errors under nonlocal coupling. Beyond the algorithmic design, we establish a fully discrete error analysis for the L1–WSLD discretization. We prove fixed-grid stability of the discrete operator and show that the discrete approximation error is controlled by the L1–WSLD consistency defect and the square root of the physical residual loss.  Numerical experiments in one-, two-, and three-dimensional cases demonstrate that the proposed method achieves substantially higher accuracy than existing fPINN variants, especially for problems with simultaneous initial singularity, boundary irregularity, and multiscale features.
